  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 813 PM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nThe upper level ridge over much of the central and southeastern US \nwill persist into this weekend; however, it looks like an upper \ntrough tries to dig south over the Northeast, potentially breaking \ndown the ridge over the Appalachians, Ohio Valley, and Tennessee \nValley somewhat during this time. This may be short-lived, since \nsynoptic guidance suggests that the ridge will build back over the \nSoutheast through early next week. At the surface, high pressure over\nthe Appalachians may be overtaken by a backdoor cold front slated to\nmove into the region from the northeast by Sunday. Some guidance \nhints that this feature may bring enough moisture and lift with it to\nproduce at least a low chance of showers and storms this weekend, \nbut these trends will be monitored. By early next week, surface high \npressure builds back over the Appalachians as the upper ridge takes \nhold over the region once again. \n\nOverall, the hot and humid conditions are expected to continue \nthrough the long term period. Expect highs in the mid to upper 90s \nthrough the weekend with not much relief at night as lows merely dip \ninto the upper 60s to lower 70s. These temperatures, along with dew \npoints in the lower to mid 70s, will result in heat indices in the \n100-106 degree range along with WBGTs in the mid to upper 80s (some \nspots around 90 degrees) and Moderate to Major HeatRisk (levels 2 and\n3 of 4) for most of north Alabama and southern middle Tennessee. By \nearly next week, the aforementioned backdoor front may temper the \nheat and humidity slightly. Highs in the lower to mid 90s with dew \npoints in the upper 60s to lower 70s are anticipated, keeping the \nHeatRisk in the Moderate Risk category, heat indices generally \nbetween 98-103 degrees, and WBGTs solidly in the 83-87 degree range. \nUltimately, the current Heat Advisory runs through Saturday evening; \nhowever, we will be monitoring if a further extension into Sunday \nbecomes necessary.\n\nRemember to practice heat safety, especially if you work outside or \nhave outdoor activities! Stay hydrated, take plenty of breaks in the \nshade, and never leave people or pets in vehicles!",
